---
title: Dimensions
summary: One site across a matrix of versions or locales. An axis prefixes the slug; the outline is woven per coordinate; an untranslated page falls back to the default instead of 404ing.
related:
  - pharos/outline
  - pharos/pharos
  - content/collections
---

A version is not a second site. A locale is not a second site. They are **coordinates** on the same outline. `dimensions()` wraps N outlines — one per coordinate — into a single `Outline` that `pharos()` consumes, with the coordinate parsed off the front of the slug.

> **When to use.** Reach for dimensions when the *same page* exists in several editions — `v1` / `v2` docs, `en` / `de` locales, a stable/next channel. If your versions are genuinely different content, they are just different collections; dimensions is for one structure read at several coordinates.

## An axis over collections

You declare the axes and a `weave` that sources the outline for a coordinate. Versions are just collections — hand `weave` whichever one sources each:

```ts
// src/lib/site.server.ts
import { pharos, dimensions } from 'ogygia/pharos';
import { v1, v2 } from './collections.server';

export const site = pharos({
  base: '/docs',
  outline: dimensions({
    axes: { version: { values: ['v2', 'v1'], default: 'v2', label: 'Version' } },
    weave: ({ version }) => (version === 'v1' ? v1 : v2)
  })
});
```

The **default** coordinate serves at the bare URL (`/docs/routing`); the others are prefixed (`/docs/v1/routing`). `weave` is called once per distinct coordinate and memoized, so a version's outline is built lazily on first visit.

## Fallback, not 404

The failure mode every versioned-docs site fights is a missing translation. Dimensions turns it into a non-event: an untranslated page **renders the default coordinate's content at its URL** instead of 404ing. The switcher can always link the same slug across coordinates, because the address is guaranteed to resolve. `site.doc()` reports which axis fell back, so the page can show a "not yet translated" note if it wants.

## The switcher

`site.switcher(slug)` returns the version/locale picker for the coordinate in `slug`, hrefs baked — a serializable value the `<Switcher>` component renders as a dropdown. It stays on the *same page* across a switch, landing on that page's equivalent in the chosen coordinate (or the default, by the fallback rule).

## Nav and search across the matrix

- **`nav(slug)`** — pass the slug so the tree reflects that coordinate. The [remote](/docs/pharos/remotes) threads it through.
- **`site.entries()`** — bakes the **union** across the matrix, so a page that exists in only one coordinate still prerenders there.
- **search** — indexes the *default* coordinate's canonical addresses only, so fallback pages under other coordinates don't show up as duplicate hits.

## Coordinate as data

The coordinate is threaded as data — `slug` → coordinate — never as module-global state, so it is SSR-safe under concurrent requests. You never reach for it directly; `nav`, `doc`, `switcher`, and `entries` all take the slug and derive it.

## Do / don't

- **Do** model versions and locales as axes on one outline. One structure, read at coordinates, beats N parallel sites.
- **Do** rely on the fallback. A missing translation should degrade to the default, never break the URL.
- **Don't** encode the coordinate in module state. Pass the slug; the derivation is pure and request-safe.
- **Don't** index every coordinate for search. The default's canonical set is the honest result; fallbacks are the same page.
